 * Three engines resolve prompts in this app (js/main.js in the browser, index.php
 * for ?json=1, schedule_runner.php for scheduled tasks) and their history is one
 * of drifting apart. The two PHP engines therefore share ONE set of helpers in
 * shared_logic.php, and this suite pins three things:
 *
 *   1. the helpers themselves (split / parse / format / resolve / emit / splice);
 *   2. each PHP engine's own wiring around them;
 *   3. PARITY - the same tag and the same answer must leave both engines as the
 *      same SQL text, byte for byte.
 *
 * Runs against the SHIPPED code, never a copy:
 *   - shared_logic.php opens the database on include (require_once
 *     'db_connection.php'), and schedule_runner.php claims tasks and SENDS EMAIL
 *     on include. Neither may be require()d. Named functions are lifted out of
 *     both with PHP's own tokenizer instead.
 *   - index.php's prompt pass is top-level code, not a function. Its date-range
 *     branch is cut out by its literal first and last lines and compiled into a
 *     small loop that mirrors the pass's own loop. ONE textual change is made to
 *     that fragment: `exit;` becomes a throw, so a refusal can be asserted on
 *     instead of ending the test run. Nothing else is touched.
 *
 *   php.exe -n test_daterange.php [<app dir> [<fixture.js> [--control]]]
 *
 * <fixture.js>: where to write the parity cases for the browser suite (run.ps1
 * passes it). --control adds one deliberately false assertion: the run must
 * then end "FAIL: 1".
 *
 * -n (skip php.ini) because a workstation cannot load oci8/sqlsrv. -n also drops
 * date.timezone, so this file sets it: the relative-date grammar calls date().
 */

